###
Doc comments, and option values shown where they are used.

The run of # lines directly above a function, command, ClassySK method or
skript-reflect definition is its documentation. Two styles are read, so a
file documented in another editor reads the same here:

  @param <name> <text>, @return, @deprecated, @since, @example
  #d <text>, #dp <name> <text>, #dr, #dex <code>, #ddep <reason>

A blank line between the comment and the declaration detaches it, as the
header below shows. This block is prose, not documentation: a ### block
only counts when its closing ### sits directly above a declaration.

Two hints in this file are on purpose: the stale @param on welcomeKit, and
the struck-through call to oldKit. Nothing else should be reported.
###

options:
	prefix: &8[&6Kits&8]&r
	cooldown: 10 minutes

# A section banner, separated by a blank line: not anyone's documentation.

# Gives a player the starter kit and tells them about it.
#
# @param p the player who receives the kit
# @param loud whether to announce it to everyone
# @param amount renamed to `count` long ago: this line is hinted
# @return whether the kit fitted in their inventory
# @since 1.2
# @example
#   if welcomeKit(player, false) is false:
#       send "Make some room first." to player
function welcomeKit(p: player, loud: boolean = false) :: boolean:
	give {_p} a stone sword and 16 bread
	send "{@prefix} Enjoy your kit." to {_p}
	if {_loud} is true:
		broadcast "{@prefix} %{_p}% got the starter kit."
	return true

#d Rolls a drop chance, in the #d style from another Skript extension.
#dp chance percent chance of a drop
#dr true when the drop should be given
#dex if rollDrop(25):
#dex 	drop a diamond at player
function rollDrop(chance: number) :: boolean:
	chance of {_chance}%:
		return true
	return false

# @deprecated use welcomeKit() instead, which can announce the kit
function oldKit(p: player):
	welcomeKit({_p})

# Gives yourself the starter kit. Usable once every {@cooldown}.
command /kit:
	cooldown: {@cooldown}
	trigger:
		# Hover welcomeKit: the description, both parameters with their types,
		# the return value, since and the example. Hover oldKit: struck
		# through, and the card opens with what to use instead.
		welcomeKit(player, true)
		oldKit(player)
		# Type `welcomeKit(` on a new line: each argument's hint carries its
		# @param text. After {@prefix} below, an inlay hint shows its value.
		if rollDrop(10) is true:
			send "{@prefix} Lucky you." to player
